{"items":[{"tags":["python","set","literals"],"question_score":669,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":44576113,"is_answered":false,"question_id":6130374,"item_type":"answer","score":3,"last_activity_date":1789914114,"creation_date":1497557139,"body":"Adding to the crazy ideas: with Python 3 accepting unicode identifiers, you could declare a variable  (ϕ is U+03D5 GREEK PHI SYMBOL) and use it instead.\n","excerpt":"Adding to the crazy ideas: with Python 3 accepting unicode identifiers, you could declare a variable ϕ = <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>() (ϕ is U+03D5 GREEK PHI SYMBOL) and use it instead. &hellip; ","title":"Can an empty set be written as a literal?"},{"tags":["python","caching","memoization","python-decorators"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":80002686,"is_answered":false,"question_id":17167006,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1789244760,"creation_date":1789244760,"body":"Considering  are hashable.\nFirst, We can also cache the  to reduce redundant compute time.\n\nThen, if  and  are deterministic calls based on the class state, if you want to reduce the time spent on those methods, consider making the class a frozen dataclass and make those functions cached properties. Thanks to the frozen property of the object, internal state won&#39;t be altered between calls. Without, the cache would be incorrect.\n\n\nIf you need to mutate the object and invalid the cache, forget about frozen. What you need is to detect mutation of your object and invalidate cache when it happens. This helper class will do the trick:\n\nNow, if you inherit from this helper class, your cached properties will have their cache invalidated on object mutation. Here is qn example:\n\n\n","excerpt":"This helper class will do the trick:\nimport functools\n\n\nclass PropertiesCacheInvalidation:\n    @classmethod\n    @functools.cache\n    def _cached_property_names(cls):\n        return <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>( &hellip; ","title":"Memoize a function which uses instance attributes"},{"tags":["python","python-decorators"],"question_score":4,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":60232512,"item_type":"question","score":4,"last_activity_date":1789159794,"creation_date":1581709025,"body":"I am working with Python code that calls into C wrappers, but the C code is very buggy (and I have no other alternative) and causes a segfault when a Python object managed in C goes out of scope, so I have to keep references to each object created.\n\nIs there any good way to make an ergonomic &quot;unique&quot; wrapper where each class can only have one instance per set of constructor arguments, e.g.\n\n\n\nI&#39;ve made this decorator, but it prevents any @unique-decorated class from being used as a base class (constructing an instance of the derived class calls the  of the decorator).\n\n\n","excerpt":"unique_class = unique_cls\n        def __new__(cls, *args, **kwargs):\n            if not Unique.instances.get(\n                (\n                     cls.unique_class,\n                     f_args := <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> &hellip; (args),\n                     f_kwargs := <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>(kwargs),\n                )                \n            ):\n                Unique.instances[\n                     (Unique.unique_class, f_args, f_kwargs &hellip; ","title":"Best way to make decorator for unique class instances Python"},{"tags":["python","flask","raspberry-pi"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":3,"is_answered":true,"question_id":77707533,"item_type":"question","score":3,"last_activity_date":1786977392,"creation_date":1703332529,"body":"I bought the new PI5, and I&#39;m in a middle of a difficulty:\nThis is a python/flask script - main.py - which call for a python script named shift_register.py and runs SRoutput():\n\nThis is script shift_register.py:\n\n(Sorry, the comments are in Portuguese...)\nHowever, when I run main.py I get these errors:\n\n(I&#39;ve changed all the GPIO&#39;s and they are all busy)\nStrangely enough, this worked with PI3 and RPi.\nWhat am I missing?\nThank you!\n","excerpt":"recent call last):\n  File &quot;/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/gpiozero/pins/pi.py&quot;, line 408, in pin\n    pin = self.pins[info]\n          ~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^\nKeyError: PinInfo(number=29, name=&#39;GPIO5&#39;, names=<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> &hellip; ({&#39;BOARD29&#39;, &#39;BCM5&#39;, 5, &#39;5&#39;, &#39;WPI21&#39;, &#39;GPIO5&#39;, &#39;J8:29&#39;}), pull=&#39;&#39;, row=15, col=1, interfaces=<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({&#39;&#39;, &#39;spi&#39;, &#39;dpi&#39;, &#39;gpio&#39;, &#39;i2c&#39;, &#39;uart&#39;}))\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception &hellip; ","title":"Busy GPIO&#39;s Raspberry PI5"},{"tags":["best-practices","python"],"question_score":-3,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79985484,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79984365,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1785355754,"creation_date":1784882945,"body":"I made a small library of descriptors a while back for this exact reason. You can find it on GitHub.\nI never even bothered making it a proper installable library, so just copy and paste the code somewhere. I&#39;m pretty sure the readme.md isn&#39;t even up to date.\nBasically, here is how you would use it in your case:\n\nThe idea behind this library was to explicitly define properties that could be operated on, and then define additional read-only properties through operations on the set of operable properties.\nThose result properties are lazily computed and then cached, which is preferable when computing them is expensive. I&#39;m not sure computing your  property is the best example since   is not that expensive.\nAlso, I don&#39;t recommend using my library with inheritance. It&#39;s not impossible, but it is usually more trouble than it is worth.  is usually fine.\n","excerpt":"Basically, here is how you would use it in your case:\n# Wherever you put the code\nfrom descriptors import OperableProperty as OP, Result\n\nclass AlphabetString:\n\n    istr: str = OP()\n    alphabet: <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> &hellip; I&#39;m not sure computing your alphabet property is the best example since  <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> is not that expensive.\nAlso, I don&#39;t recommend using my library with inheritance. &hellip; ","title":"What is best practice for preventing a class attribute from becoming stale data?"},{"tags":["best-practices","python"],"question_score":-3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79984365,"item_type":"question","score":-3,"last_activity_date":1785355754,"creation_date":1784844933,"body":"How do we lazily re-compute data for read-only getters, or read-only accessors?\nLet&#39;s say we have a string:\n\nNow, we compute the set of characters in that string:\n\nThirdly, someone mutates the original string. The  is now known as the , and there is at least one other mutation.\n\nWhenever a user invokes the getter (the read-only attribute access) for the set of characters in the string, then the user should receive a new set of characters for the newly updated strings:\n\nThe new set of characters should only be re-computed from the hotel description if, and when, someone asks for the set of characters.\nWhen the hotel name string mutates, then the alphabet set should remain stale for a long time.\n\n\nIdeally, when an  is mutated, then we should re-direct the high-level alphabet getter, so that a new value will be computed, and there will be no stale data.\n","excerpt":"_string      = istr\n        # TO DO:\n        #    replace <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> with a copy-on-write (COW)\n        #    wrapper around the instance of the `set` class  \n        this. &hellip; _alphabet = <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>(this. &hellip; ","title":"What is best practice for preventing a class attribute from becoming stale data?"},{"tags":["best-practices","python"],"question_score":-3,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79984366,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79984365,"item_type":"answer","score":4,"last_activity_date":1784848417,"creation_date":1784845788,"body":"You can label the fields you want to have behavior on setting, or be computed on getting, as .\nOne way to do this is to compute your internal set property when it&#39;s first accessed (that is, lazily).\n\nNow if , you can access  and get the frozenset containing  and , and it will be cached if you access it again.  But if you reassign  and then access  again it will be recomputed.  You&#39;re not allowed to try to assign  since that doesn&#39;t have a setter.\nYou also have the choice of computing the alphabet eagerly, in the constructor and in the string setter function.  This simplifies the typing a little bit (the internal  is never None) but it could waste some cycles if you&#39;re never looking up the value.\n","excerpt":"_alphabet: <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>[str] | None = None\n\n  @property\n  def alphabet(self) -&gt; <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>[str]:\n    if self._alphabet is None:\n      self._alphabet = <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>(self._string)\n    return self. &hellip; You&#39;re not allowed to try to assign astr.alphabet = <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>() since that doesn&#39;t have a setter. &hellip; ","title":"What is best practice for preventing a class attribute from becoming stale data?"},{"tags":["python","list","dictionary","duplicates"],"question_score":294,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":23358757,"is_answered":false,"question_id":9427163,"item_type":"answer","score":25,"last_activity_date":1781703538,"creation_date":1398757979,"body":"If you want to preserve the Order, then you can use OrderedDict:\n\nIf the order doesn&#39;t matter, then you can do\n\n","excerpt":"If you want to preserve the Order, then you can use OrderedDict:\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\nprint OrderedDict((<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>(d.items()), d) for d in data).values()\n# [{&#39;a&#39;: 123, &#39;b&#39;: 1234}, {&#39;a&#39;: &hellip; 3222, &#39;b&#39;: 1234}]\n\nIf the order doesn&#39;t matter, then you can do\nprint {<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>(d.items()): d for d in data}.values()\n# [{&#39;a&#39;: 3222, &#39;b&#39;: 1234}, {&#39;a&#39;: 123, &#39;b&#39;: 1234}] &hellip; ","title":"Remove duplicate dict in list in Python"},{"tags":["python","performance","python-internals"],"question_score":7,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":79945579,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79945536,"item_type":"answer","score":17,"last_activity_date":1779617685,"creation_date":1779536338,"body":"The generated bytecode is different for 3 or more literals (see the edit below for where this comes from)\n\noutputs:\n\nFor 1 or 2 literals, the values are loaded one by one, then the set gets constructed from them.\nFor 3 or more, an empty set gets created, then updated at once with a (prebuilt during parsing, I imagine?) frozenset. (The same happens for lists, with a prebuilt tuple.)\nThis explains why the speed remains the same with longer sets like , as the frozenset is in every case just \nThis also explains why we stop seeing a difference between 2 and 3 elements as soon as there is a variable among the elements of the set: no frozenset gets created, the values and variables get loaded one by one and the set created at the end, just like in the 2 literals case. The time needed then grows regularly with the number of elements.\n\n\nA good question would be: would it be faster to also use prebuilt frozensets/tuples when there are only two literals?\n\nI searched the source code for where this happens:\nin  cpython/Python/flowgraph.c, we have the description of the optimization:\n\n has been introduced in this commit, but I didn&#39;t find any trace of such a minimal value in the oldest versions of the code (I might have missed it though), and I couldn&#39;t find any discussion of this seemingly arbitrary value.\n","excerpt":"<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>. (The same happens for lists, with a prebuilt tuple.) &hellip; &quot;for&quot; loop, comprehension or &quot;in&quot;/&quot;not in&quot; tests:\n           Change literal list or set of constants into constant\n           tuple or <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> respectively. &hellip; ","title":"Why is {1,2} slower than {1,2,3}?"},{"tags":["c#",".net","regex"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79942509,"is_answered":false,"question_id":53840169,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1779029740,"creation_date":1779029740,"body":"Since .NET 8 you can use  especially for high-performance scenarios:\n\nProvides an immutable, read-only set of values optimized for efficient searching.\n\n.NET 10 version of the code would look like the following:\n\ndotnetfiddle\nSample benchmark:\n\nWhich gives on my machine:\n\n\n\nMethod\nString\nMean\nError\nStdDev\n\n\n\n\nViaHashSet\n54323.5\n15.126 ns\n0.0433 ns\n0.0361 ns\n\n\nViaFrozenSet\n54323.5\n14.048 ns\n0.0402 ns\n0.0376 ns\n\n\nViaCharsSearchValues\n54323.5\n6.156 ns\n0.0136 ns\n0.0120 ns\n\n\nViaCharsSearchValuesIndexOfAny\n54323.5\n3.418 ns\n0.0161 ns\n0.0151 ns\n\n\nViaHashSet\n543g23.5\n8.241 ns\n0.0730 ns\n0.0683 ns\n\n\nViaFrozenSet\n543g23.5\n7.152 ns\n0.0294 ns\n0.0275 ns\n\n\nViaCharsSearchValues\n543g23.5\n2.927 ns\n0.0113 ns\n0.0100 ns\n\n\nViaCharsSearchValuesIndexOfAny\n543g23.5\n1.321 ns\n0.0057 ns\n0.0051 ns\n\n\n\nNotes:\n\nAll the approaches shown in the benchmark rely on the fact that allowed chars are static or rarely change so you can build collection single time and reuse it\n relies on utilizing SIMD instructions for  and similar operations so it will give performance only if a) those are present (should be the case for quire a lot of modern CPUs especially x86 architecture) b) the input is relatively long, in my other bench with single character string  have shown worse timing compared to the iteration approach with /\n\n","excerpt":".&#39;];\n    private static <span class=\"highlight\">FrozenSet</span>&lt;char&gt; CharsFrozenset = CharsHashset.ToFrozenSet();\n    private static SearchValues&lt;char&gt; CharsSearchValues = SearchValues.Create(CharsHashset.ToArray());\n    \n    [Benchmark &hellip; modern CPUs especially x86 architecture) b) the input is relatively long, in my other bench with single character string IndexOfAny have shown worse timing compared to the iteration approach with <span class=\"highlight\">FrozenSet</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to check if a string contains chars that are outside of a given char list"},{"tags":["python"],"question_score":62,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":76937275,"is_answered":false,"question_id":6463918,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1776803207,"creation_date":1692484098,"body":"This should work for Python&#160;3.4 (and later) and can be easily imported:\n\n","excerpt":"Python &gt;= 3.4 is required to use get_stdlib, but you have {}&quot;.format(err, python_version))\n\ndef list_stdlib() -&gt; <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Get a list of the Python standard library for the current Python &hellip; if mod.suffix == &#39;.py&#39; and mod.parent == std_lib_path:\n            std_lib.add(mod.stem)\n        elif mod.is_dir() and mod.parent == std_lib_path:\n            std_lib.add(mod.stem)\n\n    return <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> &hellip; ","title":"How can I get a list of all the Python standard library modules?"},{"tags":["python","dictionary","data-structures","immutability"],"question_score":239,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":30638022,"is_answered":false,"question_id":2703599,"item_type":"answer","score":112,"last_activity_date":1776111838,"creation_date":1433404359,"body":"Python 3.15+\nThere is a built-in  type. See What&#39;s new in Python 3.15 and PEP 814 – Add frozendict built-in type.\nAlthough frozendict shares most of its code with the dict type in CPython, the new frozendict type is not a dict subclass. This is so that it doesn&#39;t inherit mutating methods such as , , . Otherwise, it behaves as you would expect from a standard mapping type (amortized O(1) lookup etc).\n\nInsertion order is preserved.\nImmutable\nKeys must be hashable, but values can be non-hashable. Using only hashable values creates a hashable frozendict.\n\nA hashable frozendict instance can be used as a set element, dict key, or passed as an argument to a function decorated with . It&#39;s also safe to use a hashable frozendict instance as a function default without causing the &quot;Least Astonishment&quot; and the Mutable Default Argument footgun.\nOnce a frozendict is created, its shallow immutability is guaranteed. This means it can be safely shared between threads without synchronization, as long as its values are not modified by other threads.\nUnlike a standard dict, the in-place union operation () will create/return a new instance rather than updating an existing instance.\nPython &lt; 3.15\nCuriously, although we have the seldom useful , there&#39;s still no frozen mapping. The idea was rejected in PEP 416 -- Add a frozendict builtin type. This idea may be revisited in a later Python release, see PEP 603 -- Adding a frozenmap type to collections.\nSo the Python 2 solution to this:\n\nStill seems to be the usual:\n\nIn Python 3 you have the option of this:\n\nNow the default config can be updated dynamically, but remain immutable where you want it to be immutable by passing around the proxy instead.\nSo changes in the  will update  as expected, but you can&#39;t write to the mapping proxy object itself.\nAdmittedly it&#39;s not really the same thing as an &quot;immutable, hashable dict&quot;, but it might be a decent substitute for some use cases of a frozendict in Python &lt; 3.15.\n","excerpt":"Python &lt; 3.15\nCuriously, although we have the seldom useful <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>, there&#39;s still no frozen mapping. The idea was rejected in PEP 416 -- Add a frozendict builtin type. &hellip; ","title":"What would a &quot;frozen dict&quot; be?"},{"tags":["python","dictionary","data-structures","immutability"],"question_score":239,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":14,"is_answered":true,"question_id":2703599,"item_type":"question","score":239,"last_activity_date":1776111838,"creation_date":1272093590,"body":"\nA frozen set is a frozenset.\nA frozen list could be a tuple.\nWhat would a frozen dict be? An immutable, hashable dict.\n\nI guess it could be something like , but that is more like a frozen-keys dict (a half-frozen dict). Isn&#39;t it?\nA &quot;frozendict&quot; should be a frozen dictionary, it should have , , , etc., and support , , etc.\n","excerpt":"A frozen set is a <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>.\nA frozen list could be a tuple.\nWhat would a frozen dict be? An immutable, hashable dict. &hellip; ","title":"What would a &quot;frozen dict&quot; be?"},{"tags":["python"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":4,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79889146,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1772791205,"creation_date":1771043478,"body":"I have the following code:\n\nI can simplify it using a dict, obviously\n\nRight now, I have something similar with sets:\n\nThe intersection of all the key sets will always be empty and  will only ever be a subset of one of them.\nI was wondering if there is a way to simplify this, or is the if-elif chain the best? I could do something with a dict from frozensets to the values and find the first one to which  is a subset using  and a generator expression, but that is getting way too &quot;clever&quot;.\nNote I&#39;m not looking for a one-liner necessarily, just if there is a better pattern for this.\n","excerpt":"I have the following code:\nif value == key1:\n    return value1\nelif value == key2:\n    return value2\n...\n\nI can simplify it using a dict, obviously\nd = { key1: value1, key2: value2, ...}\nreturn d[valu &hellip; ","title":"Map set to a value if it is a subset of a key set"},{"tags":["python","dictionary","data-structures","immutability"],"question_score":239,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":2704866,"is_answered":false,"question_id":2703599,"item_type":"answer","score":159,"last_activity_date":1772482400,"creation_date":1272120737,"body":"Python &lt; 3.15\nPython doesn&#39;t have a builtin frozendict type. It turns out this wouldn&#39;t be useful too often (though it would still probably be useful more often than  is).\nThe most common reason to want such a type is when memoizing function calls for functions with unknown arguments. The most common solution to store a hashable equivalent of a dict (where the values are hashable) is something like .\nThis depends on the sorting not being a bit insane. Python cannot positively promise sorting will result in something reasonable here. (But it can&#39;t promise much else, so don&#39;t sweat it too much.)\n\nYou could easily enough make some sort of wrapper that works much like a dict. It might look something like:\n\nThis should work great:\n\n","excerpt":"It turns out this wouldn&#39;t be useful too often (though it would still probably be useful more often than <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> is). &hellip; ","title":"What would a &quot;frozen dict&quot; be?"},{"tags":["python"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79893304,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79889146,"item_type":"answer","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1771629343,"creation_date":1771628850,"body":"As put in the comment, there is no one-liner.\nYou can put the rules in a dictionary, where the keys are frozen verions of your larger sets - but searching through then will be linear, not a fixed-time search, as happen with keys of a dictionary,\nThe fixed-time search would be possible if your  would be equal to one of the keys, but if it may be a subset of them, you are restricted to a linear search.\nOther data-structures than Python&#39;s ready-to-use dictionaries, or even a clever composition of dictionaries as indexes, could be used  for  a faster search, if needed, but unless yours to &quot;to be searched&quot; super-sets are at least a couple hundred, and you have to repeteadly do this comparison, it is not something you should worry about.\nThe pratical &quot;declarative rules&quot; is similar to what is in @Sabaresh&#39;s answer - but the keys must be frozensets:\n\n(Just to reiterate: this is worth it if you have just a few, like 3, or more super_sets in the rules. The idea to be used for  &quot;hundreds of supersets&quot; is a different thing.\n\nfaster for several super-sets:\nSo, if you have a lot of supersets, we can use the fact that there is no intersection between then to create an index dictionary and reverse the search:\n\n","excerpt":"The pratical &quot;declarative rules&quot; is similar to what is in @Sabaresh&#39;s answer - but the keys must be frozensets:\nf = <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> # just an alias shortcut\n\nrules = {\n    f({key1, key2, key3}): value_1, &hellip; ","title":"Map set to a value if it is a subset of a key set"},{"tags":["python","loops","dictionary","set","frozenset"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":3,"is_answered":true,"question_id":59010211,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1771090995,"creation_date":1574529438,"body":"I have a frozen set dictionary of the form:\n\nIs it possible for me to iterate  over the values based on one of the key from the frozenset?\nExample:\nIf I provide the value 12345, I want to return\n\nIf I provide the value 3245, I want to return\n\nBasically, I want to iterate based one of the key from the multikey frozen set dictionary\n","excerpt":"I have a frozen set dictionary of the form:\n{<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 3245}): 45.95948791503906,\n <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 12804138}): 48.996036529541016,\n <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({3245, 9876}): 50.67853927612305,\n\nIs it possible &hellip; Example:\nIf I provide the value 12345, I want to return\n<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 3245}): 45.95948791503906,\n<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 12804138}): 48.996036529541016\n\nIf I provide the value 3245, I want to return\n<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> &hellip; ","title":"Retrieve values from a dictionary based on one element in a frozen set key"},{"tags":["python","loops","dictionary","set","frozenset"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79889399,"is_answered":false,"question_id":59010211,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1771090995,"creation_date":1771090560,"body":"The naive approach is to loop over the keys and check each one if it contains what you&#39;re looking for.\n\nOutput:\n\nThis would be slow for large data since it takes O(n) time to loop over the dict. If you need lookups to be faster, you could compile a lookup dict ahead of time like in Dan D&#39;s answer. That takes O(n) time initially (plus O(n) space), but then lookups are O(1) average. Or, if you need to be able to reassign values and have each key reflect that, take a look at How do I make a dictionary with multiple keys to one value?\nP.S. Magofoco already posted the loop approach, but the code was not great.\n","excerpt":"The naive approach is to loop over the keys and check each one if it contains what you&#39;re looking for.\nd = {\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 3245}): 45.95948791503906,\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 12804138}): 48.996036529541016 &hellip; ,\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({3245, 9876}): 50.67853927612305}\n\nfindme = 12345\nfor k, v in d.items():\n    if findme in k:\n        print(k, v)\n\nOutput:\n<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 3245}) 45.95948791503906\n<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 12804138 &hellip; ","title":"Retrieve values from a dictionary based on one element in a frozen set key"},{"tags":["python","loops","dictionary","set","frozenset"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":59010587,"is_answered":false,"question_id":59010211,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1771089208,"creation_date":1574531809,"body":"If you are going to do a lot of this, you might want to transform from  to . The result is that the answers are all . If you only need to do it a little and need to keep the data in the other form, use the loop in the other answer.\nTo do this transform:\n\nThis results in  being:\n\n","excerpt":"To do this transform:\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nd = {\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 3245}): 45.95948791503906,\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 12804138}): 48.996036529541016,\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({3245, 9876}): 50.67853927612305 &hellip; ]), 50.67853927612305)],\n 9876: [(<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>([3245, 9876]), 50.67853927612305)],\n 12345: [(<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>([12345, 12804138]), 48.996036529541016),\n         (<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>([3245, 12345]), 45.95948791503906)],\n 12804138 &hellip; ","title":"Retrieve values from a dictionary based on one element in a frozen set key"},{"tags":["python","loops","dictionary","set","frozenset"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":59010321,"is_answered":false,"question_id":59010211,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1771088400,"creation_date":1574530231,"body":"You can try:\n\n","excerpt":"You can try:\nmy_dict = {\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 3245}): 45.95948791503906,\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({12345, 12804138}): 48.996036529541016,\n    <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({3245, 9876}): 50.67853927612305}\n\ndef retrieve_dict(number_I_want &hellip; ","title":"Retrieve values from a dictionary based on one element in a frozen set key"},{"tags":["python","multiset","frozenset"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":73519717,"is_answered":false,"question_id":73519535,"item_type":"answer","score":3,"last_activity_date":1770987854,"creation_date":1661698399,"body":"As @Davis Herring pointed out,  makes a set. So the FrozenMultiset treats its contents as a set would. Using tuples  or lists  instead solves the problem.\n\n","excerpt":"As @Davis Herring pointed out, {3,3} makes a set. So the FrozenMultiset treats its contents as a set would. Using tuples (3,3) or lists [3,3] instead solves the problem.\n&gt;&gt;&gt; fms((3, 4, 3, 1)) == fms(( &hellip; ","title":"Why are frozen multisets in Python not considering repeated elements?"},{"tags":["python","multiset","frozenset"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":73519535,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1770987854,"creation_date":1661696927,"body":"I am working with expression trees which are equivalent to each other considering commutativity. To bring about this equivalence, I thought I will use frozen multisets for holding the operands as they are hashable (unlike dicts), unordered (unlike tuples), and allow for repeated elements (unlike sets).\nBut the frozen multisets don&#39;t seem to work as I imagined. They are treating repeated elements as a single element.\n\nAm I missing something here?\nOr please suggest alternatives which serve my purpose.\n","excerpt":"I am working with expression trees which are equivalent to each other considering commutativity. To bring about this equivalence, I thought I will use frozen multisets for holding the operands as they &hellip; ","title":"Why are frozen multisets in Python not considering repeated elements?"},{"tags":["python","c++","enums","bidirectional"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":75570512,"is_answered":false,"question_id":66033893,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1770282351,"creation_date":1677395911,"body":"It can be done without processing, by sharing common file:\nshared.py\n\nWhen shared.py used with Python, all lines starts with  are ignored as they Python comments. When it used with C, the Python code in the  is ignored and the  statements take effect\nmain.c\n\nmain.py\n\nEdit\nWay to write comments in this syntax:\n\n","excerpt":"__VA_ARGS__\n\n#if 0\ndef statement_end():\n    return\n#endif\n\nmy_table = {<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({&quot;OK&quot; , 0}), <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({&quot;ERROR&quot;, 1}), <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>({&quot;UNKNOWN&quot;, -1})}\n\nstatement_end()\n\n#undef my_table\n#undef statement_end &hellip; #undef <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>\n\nWhen shared.py used with Python, all lines starts with # are ignored as they Python comments. &hellip; ","title":"Efficient, DRY bi-directional lookup in both python and C++"},{"tags":["python","immutability"],"question_score":273,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79752771,"is_answered":false,"question_id":4828080,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1769051887,"creation_date":1756747122,"body":"Using an immutable type (like  or ) as a base class works, but brings with it extra methods that you might not want.\nYou can instead use a class with zero slots. This seems kind of useless--What&#39;s the point of having an instance if it can&#39;t have any data?--but it does carry one piece of information: the instance identity itself. This can be used as a lookup key. But if you want it to clean itself up properly, you should include the  slot so you can use a  instead.\n\nYou won&#39;t be able to set any new attributes on the instances because of the , and even  is not writable.\nThat doesn&#39;t mean the instance data is impossible to change. There&#39;s nothing to stop you from replacing an instance&#39;s entry in . And the  attribute is also writable. But a class object based on  can also be modified, which could affect any of its instances in arbitrary ways.\n","excerpt":"Using an immutable type (like tuple or <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>) as a base class works, but brings with it extra methods that you might not want.\nYou can instead use a class with zero slots. &hellip; ","title":"How to make an immutable object in Python?"},{"tags":["set","best-practices"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79867442,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1768371972,"creation_date":1768359392,"body":"What functional or mixed functional programming languages support nested sets, akin to the usage in mathematics and set theory?  Specifically those with a REPL?\nI tried Python and couldn&#39;t get nested sets to easily work.  I tried frozenset() and set().\n","excerpt":"I tried <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span>() and set(). &hellip; ","title":"Nested Sets: which languages support this?"},{"tags":["python","hash","set","python-internals"],"question_score":37,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":20833285,"is_answered":false,"question_id":20832279,"item_type":"answer","score":35,"last_activity_date":1767338870,"creation_date":1388377084,"body":"This was written before the answer by Raymond Hettinger (the code&#39;s author) was posted\n\nThere&#39;s usually less &quot;science&quot; in these things than you might expect: you take some general principles, and a test suite, and fiddle the constants almost arbitrarily until the results look &quot;good enough&quot;.\nSome general principles &quot;obviously&quot; at work here:\n\nTo get the desired quick &quot;bit dispersion&quot;, you want to multiply by a large integer.  Since CPython&#39;s hash result has to fit in 32 bits on many platforms, an integer that requires 32 bits is best for this.  And, indeed, .\n\nTo avoid systematically losing the low-order bit(s), you want to multiply by an odd integer.  3644798167 is odd.\n\nMore generally, to avoid compounding patterns in the input hashes, you want to multiply by a prime.  And 3644798167 is prime.\n\nAnd you also want a multiplier whose binary representation doesn&#39;t have obvious repeating patterns.  .  That&#39;s pretty messed up, which is a good thing ;-)\n\n\nThe other constants look utterly arbitrary to me.  The\n\npart is needed for another reason:  internally, CPython takes a  return value from an integer-valued C function as meaning &quot;an exception needs to be raised&quot;; i.e., it&#39;s an error return.  So you&#39;ll never see a hash code of  for any object in CPython.  The value returned instead of  is wholly arbitrary - it just needs to be the same value (instead of -1) each time.\nI don&#39;t know what Raymond used to test this. Here&#39;s what I would have used: look at hash statistics for all subsets of a set of consecutive integers. Those are problematic because  for a great many integers .\n\nSimply xor&#39;ing hashes together will yield massive cancellation on inputs like that.\nSo here&#39;s a little function to generate all subsets, and another to do a dirt-simple xor across all hash codes:\n\nThen a driver, and a little function to display collision statistics:\n\nUsing the dirt-simple hasher is disastrous:\n\nYikes! OTOH, using the  designed for frozensets does a perfect job in this case:\n\nThen you can play with that to see what does - and doesn&#39;t - make a real difference in .  For example, it still does a perfect job on these inputs if\n\nis removed.  And I have no idea why that line is there. Similarly, it continues to do a perfect job on these inputs if the  in the inner loop is removed - don&#39;t know why that&#39;s there either. And initialization can be changed from:\n\nto:\n\nwithout harm here too.  That all jibes with what I expected: it&#39;s the multiplicative constant in the inner loop that&#39;s crucial, for reasons already explained.  For example, add 1 to it (use 3644798168) and then it&#39;s no longer prime or odd, and the stats degrade to:\n\nStill quite usable, but definitely worse.  Change it to a small prime, like 13, and it&#39;s worse:\n\nUse a multiplier with an obvious binary pattern, like , and worse again:\n\nPlay around! These things are fun :-)\n","excerpt":"This was written before the answer by Raymond Hettinger (the code&#39;s author) was posted\n\nThere&#39;s usually less &quot;science&quot; in these things than you might expect: you take some general principles, and a te &hellip; ","title":"Python frozenset hashing algorithm / implementation"},{"tags":["python","hash","set","python-internals"],"question_score":37,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":3,"is_answered":true,"question_id":20832279,"item_type":"question","score":37,"last_activity_date":1767338870,"creation_date":1388368461,"body":"I&#39;m currently trying to understand the mechanism behind the hash function defined for Python&#39;s built-in  data type.  The implementation is shown at the bottom for reference.  What I&#39;m interested in particular is the rationale for the choice of this scattering operation:\n\n\n\nwhere  is the hash of each element.  Does anyone know where these came from?  (That is, was there any particular reason to pick these numbers?)  Or were they simply chosen arbitrarily?\n\n\n\nHere is the snippet from the official CPython implementation,\n\n\n\nand an equivalent implementation in Python:\n\n\n","excerpt":"I&#39;m currently trying to understand the mechanism behind the hash function defined for Python&#39;s built-in <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> data type.  The implementation is shown at the bottom for reference. &hellip; ","title":"Python frozenset hashing algorithm / implementation"},{"tags":["python","python-typing"],"question_score":24,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":77688550,"is_answered":false,"question_id":63104689,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1766869655,"creation_date":1703029404,"body":"I developped a package  (GH), which provides a  type.\nIt provides both a  constructor and a  function, either of which will get a repeatable iterable from any iterable:\n\nCurrently, it &quot;natively&quot; considers any of the following objects a : , , , , , , , , , , , , . Anything else will be passed into  before being returned.\nPlease submit a PR if you see other useful cases in Python standard library.\n","excerpt":")\ntasks2 = RepeatableIterable(tasks, (QuerySet,))\n\nCurrently, it &quot;natively&quot; considers any of the following objects a RepeatableIterable: list, tuple, range, str, bytes, bytearray, memoryview, set, <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> &hellip; ","title":"What is the pythonic way to represent an Iterable that can be iterated over multiple times"},{"tags":["c#","xmlserializer","xml-deserialization"],"question_score":5,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79848772,"item_type":"question","score":5,"last_activity_date":1765920699,"creation_date":1765906738,"body":"Given a class like this:\n\nand this XML:\n\nI&#39;m trying to deserialize this as-is, but my code throws an exception:\n\nSystem.InvalidOperationException - you must implement a default accessor on System.Collections.Frozen.FrozenSet`1 [...] because it inherits from ICollection.\n\nChanging  to a  allows me to deserialize just fine as expected. I attempted using a  as a proxy for the frozen set member:\n\nbut this yields another exception:\n\nInvalidOperationException: there is an error in XML document (3, 8) [...] ReadElementContentAs() methods cannot be called on an element that has child elements. Line 3, position 8\n\nI don&#39;t want to use a regular hash set because this code is used for a program settings object. These settings will be read across multiple threads and many times (at least 100,000 checks), so the extra speed that  offers over  is appreciated.\nUsing the suggestion here (wrapping the HashSet in another class and using that as the proxy type for the target frozen set) allowed me to get something working, but it makes the XML and code a little more clunky than I like.\nOther questions focus on non-frozen collections, which don&#39;t solve the problems which seem to be unique to frozen collections.\nFull, most basic, runnable code sample:\n\n","excerpt":"These settings will be read across multiple threads and many times (at least 100,000 checks), so the extra speed that <span class=\"highlight\">FrozenSet</span> offers over HashSet is appreciated. &hellip; ;\n\nforeach (int i in a.Foo)\n    Console.WriteLine(i);\n\npublic class A\n{\n    public <span class=\"highlight\">FrozenSet</span>&lt;int&gt; Foo { get; set; }\n} &hellip; ","title":"Deserialize XML List into FrozenSet"},{"tags":["python","types","immutability","mutable"],"question_score":213,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":77177223,"is_answered":false,"question_id":8056130,"item_type":"answer","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1765560394,"creation_date":1695706122,"body":"Update: disregard my erroneous points in this answer. All variables are passed by reference. I&#39;ll fix this answer later. See this comment, and the comments below my answer: Immutable vs Mutable types\nI&#39;m choosing not to delete it while I fix it up so that I can get more feedback and corrections in the comments first.\n\nMutable types vs immutable types in Python\n1. Summary\n\nMutable in plain English means changeable. Think: &quot;mutation&quot;.\n\nImmutable means unchangeable.\n\nPython has no concept of constants. Immutable vs mutable does not mean constant vs not constant, respectively. Rather, it means immutable --&gt; shared memory (a single underlying object in memory via dynamic memory allocation, for a given literal value assigned to variables) vs mutable --&gt; not shared memory (multiple underlying objects in memory via dynamic memory allocation, for a given literal value assigned to variables), respectively. I have more on this below, as this is quite nuanced.\n\nThis, as a consequence, also means pass by reference (mutable) vs pass by value (immutable), since objects which are capable of maintaining their own unique underlying objects in memory can pass those mutable memory chunks by reference so that they can be mutated.\n\n\nEverything in Python is an object. Even numbers, integers, float, etc. are objects. All variables are objects.\n\nThe mutable vs immutable objects types in Python are listed below.\n\nMutable types are passed by reference, and cause side effects.\n\nIf you do , then change , it does also change  and . This is a side effect. It is because each variable points to the same underlying object (memory blob).\n\nAgain, if you chain-assign mutable types, each mutable variable points to the same underlying object in memory, since the value is passed from one variable to the next by reference:\n\nTherefore, the following are all :\n\n\nIf you independently assign the same literal value to mutable types, however, each mutable variable points to its own underlying object in memory, since it is expected to be able to independently mutate the memory it points to:\n\n\nIf you pass a mutable variable to a function, and that function modifies it, the modification will automatically be seen outside the function. This is a side effect:\n\n\nTo force a mutable type to be passed by value instead of by reference, you can call the  method to force a copy of the underlying object to be made.\n\n\n\n\nImmutable types are passed by copy, and do not cause side effects.\n\nIf you do , then change , it does not change  or , because that would be a side effect. It has no side effects.\n\nHowever, if you assign the same value to multiple immutable variables, whether by chain assignment or independent literal assignment, the variables are both equal ( is ) and they are the same ( is ), as shown here:\n\nThat&#39;s a bit confusing, but the immutability and lack of side effects holds true.\n\nIf you pass an immutable variable to a function, and that function modifies it, the modification will not be seen outside the function. There are no side effects. Rather, if you want to see the change outside the function, you must return the modified variable from the function, and reassign it outside the function.\n\n\nTo force an immutable type to be passed by reference instead of by copy, you can simply wrap the immutable type inside of a mutable type, such as a list, and then pass the mutable wrapper to a function. This way, it gets passed by reference, and the side effect of modifying its contents is automatically visible outside the function:\n\nFor single numbers, however, it&#39;s clearer to just use the return value method above, instead: .\n\n\n\nYou can write a program to automatically identify whether a type is mutable or immutable by looking for and identifying side effects. I do this below.\n\nPython is not an easy programming language. It has tons of nuances like this. It&#39;s just popular, different, and very high level.\n\n\nAll of the test code for the above learning is below.\n2. List of mutable vs immutable objects in Python\nHere&#39;s a list of the most common mutable and immutable objects in Python. This list can be obtained by 1) painstakingly searching Python&#39;s official &quot;Built-in Types&quot; reference page for the words &quot;mutable&quot; and &quot;immutable&quot;. I verified and updated the list below based on my own findings, and added all quotes, mostly from the official documentation.\nMutable objects:\n\nlist - &quot;Lists are mutable sequences&quot;\nset - &quot;The set type is mutable&quot;\ndict - &quot;A mapping object maps hashable values to arbitrary objects. Mappings are mutable objects. There is currently only one standard mapping type, the dictionary.&quot;\nbytearray\n\n&quot;As bytearray objects are mutable, they support the mutable sequence operations in addition to the common bytes and bytearray operations&quot;\n&quot;bytearray objects are mutable and have an efficient overallocation mechanism&quot;\n&quot;bytearray objects are a mutable counterpart to bytes objects.&quot;\n\n\n\nImmutable objects:\n\nAll numeric types. The official Python docs say nothing about their immutability, but every source I find confirms they are immutable, as does my personal testing below. From RealPython.com: &quot;Numeric types...are immutable.&quot;\n\nint\nfloat\ncomplex\nbool\n\n\nstr - &quot;Strings are immutable sequences of Unicode code points.&quot;\nbytes - &quot;Bytes objects are immutable sequences of single bytes.&quot;\ntuple - &quot;Tuples are immutable sequences, typically used to store collections of heterogeneous data&quot;\nfrozenset - &quot;The frozenset type is immutable and hashable&quot;\nrange - &quot;The range type represents an immutable sequence of numbers and is commonly used for looping a specific number of times in for loops.&quot;\n\n\nStop here if you like. The above is the most important.\n\n3. Reassignment\nAll variables can be reassigned in Python, whether they were previously assigned to mutable or immutable types. However, the behavior of reassignment is different for mutable and immutable types, and cannot be thought of purely in traditional C and C++-like memory terms and understanding. Python is Python, and Python is different.\nComing from C and C++ as my primary languages, the concept of &quot;mutability&quot; in Python is quite confusing. For this and other reasons, I do not consider Python to be an &quot;easy&quot; or &quot;beginner&quot; language. It is simply a very powerful &quot;extra high-level&quot; language, is all. It has a ton of very nuanced and confusing points. C is more straight-forward and concrete in this regard. (And C++ is just nuts).\nIn C and C++, my mental model of each variable is that it is a chunk of bytes in memory. When you do this:\n\n...you are changing the bytes in &#39;s chunk of memory from bits storing a , to bits storing a . You have &quot;mutated&quot; the memory allocated by that variable, meaning: the chunk of memory which is set apart for it. The type of the variable simply specifies the &quot;lens&quot; (think: looking through a magic glass lens that interprets bits into numbers, letters, etc.), or interpretation algorithm, through which you will interpret those bits (ex: , , , etc.).\nIn Python, however, that is not the mental model you should have for variables. In Python, think of variables as objects containing pointers to other objects, where everything is an object, and each object contains a bit specifying whether it is mutable or immutable, and mutable variables are passed by reference whereas immutable variables are passed by value. Note also that an object is a very sophisticated dynamically-allocated instance of a class, which manages its own state and chunk of memory, kind of like a std container in C++.\nYou can instantly see that types , , , etc. are all classes (objects) in Python, not just trivial types like they are in C and C++, by doing this:\n\nSo, in Python, when you do this:\n\nIn the case of mutable objects, the reassignment modifies its bytes in-place rather than dynamically creating a new object and pointing the variable to it. For example:\n\nThe details of how that &quot;immutable&quot; vs &quot;mutable&quot; characteristic is carried out aren&#39;t really important to the Python programmer like they would be to a low-level embedded systems C and C++ programmer like myself. Rather, it&#39;s sort of &quot;hand-wavy&quot;. The Python programmer is supposed to just blindly accept it and move on. Some high-level C++ programmers are this way too. It&#39;s a mindset thing.\nSo, as long as you know the summary in section 1 above, and the list of mutable vs immutable objects in Python in section 2 above, you are good.\n4. Pass by reference vs pass by copy, and side effects\nWhat a good Python programmer does need to know, however, is whether or not their particular variable type will be passed by reference or by copy when passed to a function. This is a very important distinction, as passing by reference causes side effects, which means that changes to the variable in one place will result to changes to that or other variables in other places, such as inside vs outside a function. This is a very important concept in Python, and to me is the main reason why the distinction between mutable and immutable types is important. I don&#39;t really care how they work under the hood otherwise.\nAgain:\n\nPassing a mutable type to a function causes side effects, which means modifications to that variable inside the function will be seen outside the function.\nPassing an immutable type to a function does not cause side effects, which means modifications to that variable inside the function will not be seen outside the function.\n\n5.  vs \nThe operator  tests for equality of values stored within a variable. The operator , I think, is used to test if two variables refer to the same object in memory. Again, the nuances of how Python does this under-the-hood are beyond me. See my section 1 summary, however, for a bunch of nuanced cases of  vs .\nThe official Python documentation describes the  and  operators under the &quot;Identity comparisons&quot; section of its documentation here: https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#is-not:\n\nThe operators  and  test for an object’s identity:  is true if and only if  and  are the same object. An Object’s identity is determined using the  function.  yields the inverse truth value.\n\nThe  function says: https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#id:\n\nReturn the “identity” of an object. This is an integer which is guaranteed to be unique and constant for this object during its lifetime. Two objects with non-overlapping lifetimes may have the same  value.\n\nSo, I interpret this to mean that the  is a unique dynamic memory address, or index into a map to one, which is assigned to each object at object creation. When you do , the  of all 4 of those parts is the same:\n\nSo, they all appear to be the same underlying object, or blob of memory.\n6. Some test code\nHere&#39;s my test code. Nearly all of the behaviors in my test code were unpredictable to me, since Python is such a dramatically different language from C and C++, and I was unable guess the results of most of these tests prior to running them and doing this learning. I had just about everything wrong.\nmutable_vs_immutable_types.py from my eRCaGuy_hello_world repo.\nNote: the test code is too long to paste in this answer, as the Stack Overflow limit is 30000 chars. So, see it at the link above.\nSample run and output:\n\nReferences\n\nNearly all of the references are my links and test code above.\n\n","excerpt":"sequences of Unicode code points.&quot;\nbytes - &quot;Bytes objects are immutable sequences of single bytes.&quot;\ntuple - &quot;Tuples are immutable sequences, typically used to store collections of heterogeneous data&quot;\n<span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> &hellip; - &quot;The <span class=\"highlight\">frozenset</span> type is immutable and hashable&quot;\nrange - &quot;The range type represents an immutable sequence of numbers and is commonly used for looping a specific number of times in for loops.&quot; &hellip; ","title":"Immutable vs Mutable types"}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":224}